WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.459
Rust application security is a layered
engineering practice.

2
00:00:03.539 --> 00:00:07.057
Use ownership, borrowing, lifetimes, and
the type system intentionally,

3
00:00:07.137 --> 00:00:10.431
but do not confuse memory safety with
complete application security.

4
00:00:10.511 --> 00:00:13.470
Keep unsafe code small, justified,
documented, and reviewed.

5
00:00:13.550 --> 00:00:15.783
Treat FFI, raw pointers, global mutable
state,

6
00:00:15.863 --> 00:00:18.543
and low-level boundaries as
security-sensitive areas.

7
00:00:18.623 --> 00:00:22.085
Treat external input as untrusted until it
has been parsed and validated.

8
00:00:22.165 --> 00:00:24.398
Use explicit types and validation
boundaries.

9
00:00:24.478 --> 00:00:28.107
Handle errors deliberately, avoid
unnecessary panics in production paths,

10
00:00:28.187 --> 00:00:32.263
and keep error messages useful without
exposing secrets or sensitive implementation

11
00:00:32.343 --> 00:00:32.734
detail.

12
00:00:32.814 --> 00:00:36.164
Protect configuration and secrets from
source code, logs, artifacts,

13
00:00:36.244 --> 00:00:37.751
and accidental serialization.

14
00:00:37.831 --> 00:00:40.567
Review crates, feature flags, transitive
dependencies,

15
00:00:40.647 --> 00:00:43.104
and build-time behavior as part of the
application.

16
00:00:43.184 --> 00:00:45.473
Keep dependency records and update
discipline.

17
00:00:45.553 --> 00:00:48.959
Design concurrency and async behavior with
resource limits, timeouts,

18
00:00:49.039 --> 00:00:51.719
cancellation, backpressure, and failure
modes in mind.

19
00:00:51.799 --> 00:00:54.702
Availability and reliability are part of
the security story.

20
00:00:54.782 --> 00:00:58.467
Finally, test security-relevant behavior,
log safely, monitor operations,

21
00:00:58.547 --> 00:00:59.552
and harden releases.

22
00:00:59.632 --> 00:01:02.982
Review build profiles, feature selections,
dependency inventories,

23
00:01:03.062 --> 00:01:05.798
configuration defaults, and deployment
documentation.

24
00:01:05.878 --> 00:01:09.060
Improve based on incidents, dependency
changes, review findings,

25
00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:10.424
and operational feedback.

26
00:01:10.504 --> 00:01:14.078
The goal is to use Rust's strengths while
engineering the whole application

27
00:01:14.158 --> 00:01:14.660
securely.
